OS-9/68000 FREEWARE COLLECTION==============================Over a thousand community programs for OS-9/68000, with their source anddocumentation where they survived, and where each one came from.Microware Systems Corporation, from the heart of Iowa, wrote OS-9 at theturn of the 1980s for Motorola's 6809, and brought it to the 68000 in1983.  Its claim to fame is a real-time, multitasking, Unix-like system onmachines far too small for Unix, built from compact modules that run ashappily from ROM as from disk.  That made it at home in the Tandy ColorComputer, Fujitsu's FM computers and the Philips CD-i player, where it ranas CD-RTOS, and on factory floors, in instruments and in traffic signals.It never stopped: OS-9 is still developed, sold and supported by MicrowareLP, at www.microware.com.This collection is the community's work, not Microware's, and Microwaredoes not endorse it.  Microware graciously gave permission for the runtimemodules its programs need -- cio, csl, csl020, fpu, math and math881 -- toship on this disk.  SOURCES.txt records it.FINDING THINGS    man <name>        everything this disk has about a program    man -k <word>     the programs about <word>    DOC/CATEGORIES    every program, by what it is for    DOC/INDEX         every program, A to Z    DOC/START-HERE    fifteen that run with nothing set upSTARTING ITOn your own OS-9 system, give this disk a drive of its own, named both /ddand /h0.  Your system goes on /h1.  Then    bash /dd/SYS/loginsets PATH, HOME, TERM and TERMCAP, and commands go by name:    cookie    soundex </dd/SYS/motdWe have not done this on hardware ourselves.  DOC/README-RUNNING has whatis known.With os9exec, a community emulator for macOS, Linux and Windows, no OS-9hardware is needed:    OS9DISK=/path/to/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=/path/to/osk-freeware.dd \    OS9H1=/path/to/your/os9  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginOS9H1 is your own licensed OS-9 system, a disk image or a directory, andappears as /h1.  Most of the collection runs without it; the programs thatwant your runb, shell or compiler find them there.  Run theimage itself, not a copy unpacked into a directory: permissions and recordlocking work only on the image.KEEPING WHAT YOU LIKE    keep cookie       copies cookie, and the files it reads, to your /h1    kept              lists what you have kept    unkeep cookie     removes it, except files you have changedDOC/README-KEEP has more.WHERE TO LOOK    DOC/README-DOCS     every way to find out how to use something    DOC/README-RUNNING  arranging /dd, /h0 and /h1; DOS disks; GNU chess    DOC/STATUS          what runs, and what does not    DOC/ORIGINS         where each program came from    DOC/README-CIO      the starred programs    DOC/README-BUSERR   a bus error is usually the wrong terminal    DOC/README-MODULES  the files in CMDS that are not commands    DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT   math, math881 and fpu    SOURCES.txt         terms, build method, and what was left out and whyTERMSEach program keeps its author's terms; SOURCES.txt records them.  Somecarry a condition their authors asked for -- no military use, or peacefulapplications only -- and SOURCES.txt lists those.  Please pass the termson with the software.